tion engineering. The focus is on mitigating potential health risks that arise in manufacturing and staffing that can lead to inadequate or reduced workplace safety management frameworks within manufacturers. Hence, the beneficial results are eliminating health risks such as noise exposure or the risks posed by toxic emissions (Azmi and Ton, 2018).\n\nNevertheless, one of the most significant impacts of automation on productivity is to increase its efficiency, which, in turn, reduces costs by leaving computerized production systems handling repetitive or time-consuming tasks, allowing employees to focus on more critical operations. This outcomes in a better quality output and minimized programming resources through constant surveillance while keeping high control standards in place.\n\nOnce more, automation's additional benefit lies in itsitizen in advancing multiple technological fields, such as manufacturing and service industry sectors. These are cultivar evolving industry areas to become more interconnected and improve integration between technological processes (Gupta, Bhattacharya, and Purkait, 2014). As such, developing high-tech technologies to improve safety standards in advanced manufacturing processes, such as ensuring a more compatible environment for industrial quality control and surfacing new hazardous chemical exposures.\n\nIn summary, automation is more crucial and necessary for firms to drive efficiencies and productivity in various industrial operations, and the benefits are translatable across essential to different sectors (Alrawabie, Alshehri, Jones, & Ellingsen, 2020). The transformative concept of automation helps businesses to achieve greater efficiency, maximize production margins, and accomplish extensive longevity of the company 。\n\nutomation is the process of employing high-advanced technology with intelligent software and robotics to control a vast number of machines and complicated processes in different sectors. The central aim of automation is to ensure ultimate efficiency and safety.\n\nAt the core of automation are electronic controls with feedback mechanisms, including systems able to respond to unexpected outcomes and adjust in a manner that achieves desired performance. In various industrial settings, including manufacturing, automating machinery and processes refers to the application of computers, automation, and systems for increased efficiency.\n\nA key feature of automation is the ability to mitigate exposure risks to hazards such as noise, oxygen, and energy, creating a better-safe working environment. Automation systems are often designed with safety features, including automatic shut-off mechanisms for machines, oxygen monitoring systems, and alerts for any unusual activity that prevents humans from being exposed to hazardous conditions.\n\nIn manufacturing, automation has considerably improved productivity, accelerated production rates, and enhanced quality control results. It has facilitated the development and management of controlled manufacturing processes by using technologies within industrial settings. The automation cells coupled with data analysis tools have been instrumental in identifying and eliminating errors in production concerning safety, fuel efficiency, or quality.\n\nThe integration of advanced technologies has been vital in driving advancements in lean manufacturing, digitization, and robotics. By enabling seamless intranet processes and collaborations among companies, there has been an accelerated innovation and faster operational efficiencies across sectors.\n\nIn conclusion, automation has transformed manufacturing and other industrial settings by enhancing efficiency, safety, and quality control. The process has been transformative in terms of harnessing technological advances to improve productivity, minimize exposure risks, and perpetually generate safer work environments 。\n\nutomation is the process of employing computer technology and robotics that co